    "name":  "Bone metastases",
    "module":  "oncology",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Bone metastases occur when cancer cells from a primary tumour spread haematogenously to the skeleton. The most common primaries producing bone metastases are breast, prostate, lung, kidney, and thyroid cancers. The axial skeleton (spine, pelvis, ribs) and proximal long bones are most frequently affected. Metastases may be osteolytic (bone-destroying, e.g., lung, kidney, breast), osteoblastic (bone-forming, e.g., prostate), or mixed. Complications include bone pain, hypercalcaemia, pathological fractures, and malignant spinal cord compression — the last of which is an oncological emergency requiring same-day imaging and treatment.",
